# modules/network/checks-tlsport.rules - the NET-08 script-check registry
# (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5). Owned by
# modules/network/tlsport.sh; ids are all `NET-TLS-*`.
#
# ONE FILE PER OWNING PHASE SCRIPT, per rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9's
# `checks-<name>.rules` row (docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 29) - modules/network/
# already carries no shared `checks.rules` for its script checks to collide
# on, so this ticket seeds directly under its own per-owner name, mirroring
# modules/network/checks-reachability.rules (NET-06).
#
# SIX IDS, ONE PER PROPERTY, THE IDENTICAL SET modules/dast/passive/
# checks-tls.rules (DAST-TLS-*) ALREADY DEFINES - because this check reuses
# that module's tls_engine.sh VERBATIM. Certificate,
# SANs, negotiated protocol and cipher on an arbitrary listener is genuinely
# new coverage - DAST-TLS-* only ever looks at the base-url listener.
# The `net` fingerprint location profile (lib/findings.sh, target host port
# transport) carries no component naming which TLS property a finding is
# about, so the check id is the ONLY thing that keeps an expired certificate
# and a weak protocol on the same listener from colliding into one finding -
# the identical reasoning checks-tls.rules' own header gives, applied one
# module down.
#
# `coverage-scope: target` is NET's cell (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5.1's own
# NET row) - a run scoped to one target can never manufacture a `fixed`
# verdict for a target it never visited.
#
# THE TYPE TAG IS `passive`, MATCHING modules/network/engine.sh's OWN
# `tlsport.sh:passive` PHASE-TABLE FLOOR.  One TLS handshake per declared
# non-base-url listener that a prior TCP classification found `open`,
# no application data sent,
# and nothing on the target changes.
#
# `quick` and `compliance` are on every record here, matching
# checks-tls.rules' own reasoning verbatim: `quick` because the family costs
# at most one handshake PER LISTENER regardless of how many checks fire, and
# `compliance` because certificate validity and protocol/cipher strength are
# transport controls every framework in docs/DESIGN.md §8.7's list
# enumerates - except NET-TLS-WILDCARD_CERT-01, which checks-tls.rules'
# DAST-TLS-WILDCARD_CERT-01 record also leaves off `compliance` for the
# identical reason (a wildcard certificate is not itself a framework control
# failure the way an expired or self-signed one is).

id: NET-TLS-WEAK_PROTOCOL-01
title: TLS endpoint on a declared listener negotiated a deprecated protocol version
script: tlsport.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-327
owasp: A02:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: target
requires-cmd: openssl
remediation: Disable SSLv2, SSLv3, TLS 1.0 and TLS 1.1 on this listener and
  offer TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3 only (RFC 8996). Where a client genuinely cannot be
  upgraded, terminate its traffic on a separate listener with its own hostname
  so the deprecated version is not offered to every client of this one.
references: CWE-327
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/
references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8996

id: NET-TLS-WEAK_CIPHER-01
title: TLS endpoint on a declared listener negotiated a weak cipher suite
script: tlsport.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-327
owasp: A02:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: target
requires-cmd: openssl
remediation: Restrict the listener cipher list to AEAD suites with forward
  secrecy (ECDHE or DHE key exchange with AES-GCM or ChaCha20-Poly1305). Remove
  every NULL, anonymous, export, RC4, single-DES, 3DES, IDEA, SEED and MD5
  suite. On TLS 1.3 leave the default suite set alone and fix the TLS 1.2 list
  instead.
references: CWE-327
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/

id: NET-TLS-CERT_EXPIRED-01
title: TLS certificate on a declared listener has expired
script: tlsport.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-324
owasp: A02:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: target
requires-cmd: openssl
remediation: Renew the certificate and reload the listener. Automate renewal
  (ACME or the platform certificate manager) and alert on the remaining
  lifetime, so the next renewal is not a manual step that can be missed again.
references: CWE-324
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/

id: NET-TLS-CERT_EXPIRING-01
title: TLS certificate on a declared listener expires soon
script: tlsport.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-324
owasp: A02:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: target
requires-cmd: openssl
remediation: Renew the certificate before it expires and confirm the listener
  picks up the new one. If renewal is already automated, check that the
  automation is running and that its own alerting fires - a silent renewal
  failure looks exactly like a working one until the day it expires. The
  warning window is config/scanner.conf's tls-expiry-warn-days, default 30.
references: CWE-324
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/

id: NET-TLS-SELF_SIGNED-01
title: TLS certificate on a declared listener is self-signed and chains to no trusted issuer
script: tlsport.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-295
owasp: A02:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: target
requires-cmd: openssl
remediation: Replace the certificate with one issued by a certificate authority
  the intended clients already trust, and serve the full intermediate chain. If
  this endpoint is internal and must use a private CA, distribute that CA to the
  clients and keep them verifying - never disable verification in the client,
  which is the change this finding usually provokes and is strictly worse than
  the certificate it works around.
references: CWE-295
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/

id: NET-TLS-WILDCARD_CERT-01
title: TLS endpoint on a declared listener serves a wildcard certificate where a host-specific one is expected
script: tlsport.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-693
owasp: A02:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: target
requires-cmd: openssl
remediation: Issue a certificate whose subjectAltName names this host
  specifically, so a compromise of its private key cannot impersonate every
  sibling host under the wildcard label. If a wildcard is genuinely the intended
  design for this target, record that by setting tls-expect-wildcard true on its
  config/scope.conf entry, which makes the expectation explicit and reviewable
  rather than making this finding disappear silently.
references: CWE-693
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/
